<body>

<!-- ── HEADER ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<div class="report-header">
    <div class="container">
        <h1>UK Retail Fuel Price Trend Analysis</h1>
        <div class="subtitle">February 2026 — Based on Government-Mandated Forecourt Price Data</div>
        <div class="meta">
            Prepared for government policy analysts and energy economists &nbsp;|&nbsp;
            Data source: UK Government Fuel Finder scheme (via fuelcosts.co.uk) &nbsp;|&nbsp;
            Report generated: {datetime.now().strftime("%Y-%m-%d")}
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="container">

<!-- ── OVERVIEW KPIs ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section class="section" id="overview">
    <h2>1. Dataset Overview & Coverage Summary</h2>
    <p>
        This analysis is based on the UK Government's open fuel price data, collected from individual forecourt
        submissions via the Fuel Finder scheme. The dataset provides a snapshot of retail pricing behaviour across
        UK petrol stations, enabling assessment of market transparency and competitiveness.
    </p>

    <div class="kpi-grid">
        <div class="kpi-card">
            <div class="kpi-value">{total_records:,}</div>
            <div class="kpi-label">Total Records</div>
        </div>
        <div class="kpi-card">
            <div class="kpi-value">{unique_stations:,}</div>
            <div class="kpi-label">Unique Stations</div>
        </div>
        <div class="kpi-card">
            <div class="kpi-value">{len(fuel_types)}</div>
            <div class="kpi-label">Fuel Types</div>
        </div>
        <div class="kpi-card">
            <div class="kpi-value">{(date_range_end - date_range_start).days}</div>
            <div class="kpi-label">Days Covered</div>
        </div>
        <div class="kpi-card">
            <div class="kpi-value">{overall_lag_median:.1f}h</div>
            <div class="kpi-label">Median Update Lag</div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Scope & Completeness</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Date range:</strong> {date_range_start.strftime('%Y-%m-%d')} to {date_range_end.strftime('%Y-%m-%d')} ({(date_range_end - date_range_start).days} days)</li>
        <li><strong>Fuel types present:</strong> {', '.join(fuel_types)}</li>
        <li><strong>Missing values:</strong> {'None detected across all columns' if all(v == 0 for v in missing_values.values()) else str(missing_values)}</li>
        <li><strong>Records per fuel type:</strong> {', '.join(f'{ft}: {fuel_type_counts[ft]:,}' for ft in fuel_order)}</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout">
        <p><strong>Note:</strong> Station identifiers are anonymised hashes; brand, geographic, and regional
        breakdowns are not available in this dataset. All prices are in pence per litre (ppl), recorded as
        integers or decimals to 0.1 ppl precision.</p>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- ── PRICE DISTRIBUTION BY FUEL TYPE ─────────────────────────────────── -->
<section class="section" id="price-by-fuel-type">
    <h2>2. Price Distribution by Fuel Type</h2>
    <p>
        This section examines the central tendency and dispersion of prices across fuel types. Understanding
        the typical price and its variation is fundamental to assessing whether consumers face consistent
        pricing or significant disparities at the pump.
    </p>

    <h3>Summary Statistics</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Fuel Type</th>
                    <th>Count</th>
                    <th>Mean</th>
                    <th>Median</th>
                    <th>Std Dev</th>
                    <th>Min</th>
                    <th>Q25</th>
                    <th>Q75</th>
                    <th>Max</th>
                    <th>IQR</th>
                    <th>Range</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>{price_table_rows}</tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <div class="chart">
        <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart_boxplot}" alt="Box plot of price distributions by fuel type">
        <div class="chart-caption">Figure 1: Box plot showing the distribution of prices for each fuel type. The box spans Q25–Q75, the line marks the median, and dots indicate outliers.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="chart">
        <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart_violin}" alt="Violin plot of price density by fuel type">
        <div class="chart-caption">Figure 2: Violin plot showing the density of prices for fuel types with sufficient observations. Blue dot = mean; red line = median.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="chart">
        <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart_mean_bar}" alt="Average price by fuel type">
        <div class="chart-caption">Figure 3: Mean price per litre for each fuel type, with values annotated.</div>
    </div>

    <h3>Key Observations</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>{most_expensive_fuel}</strong> has the highest mean price at <strong>{fmt_ppl(most_exp_mean)} ppl</strong>, while <strong>{cheapest_fuel}</strong> is the most affordable at <strong>{fmt_ppl(cheapest_mean)} ppl</strong>.</li>
        <li>The premium fuel types (B7_PREMIUM, B10) command significantly higher prices than the standard E10 and B7_STANDARD grades.</li>
        <li>Standard fuels (E10, B7_STANDARD) cluster tightly, with relatively low standard deviations, suggesting more uniform pricing across stations.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<!-- ── PRICE SPREAD & COMPETITIVENESS ──────────────────────────────────── -->
<section class="section" id="price-spread-analysis">
    <h2>3. Price Spread & Market Competitiveness</h2>
    <p>
        A competitive retail fuel market should exhibit limited price dispersion within the same fuel type.
        The interquartile range (IQR) captures the price band that 50% of consumers encounter, while the
        full max−min range reveals the total extent of pricing variation — including potential outlier
        stations charging significantly above or below the market norm.
    </p>

    <div class="chart">
        <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart_spread}" alt="Price spread: range vs IQR by fuel type">
        <div class="chart-caption">Figure 4: Comparison of max−min range and interquartile range (IQR) by fuel type. A large gap between range and IQR indicates the presence of extreme outliers.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="chart">
        <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart_histograms}" alt="Price histograms for top 3 fuel types">
        <div class="chart-caption">Figure 5: Price histograms for the three most common fuel types, showing the shape of each distribution.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout warning">
        <p><strong>Policy-Relevant Finding:</strong> The {largest_iqr_fuel} fuel type exhibits the widest
        interquartile range at <strong>{fmt_ppl(largest_iqr_val)} ppl</strong>, meaning the middle 50% of
        stations vary by this amount. For {largest_range_fuel}, the total range is
        <strong>{fmt_ppl(largest_range_val)} ppl</strong> — a consumer paying the maximum price would spend
        substantially more per litre than one at the cheapest station. This disparity warrants investigation
        into whether it reflects legitimate cost differences or poor market transparency.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Competitiveness Assessment</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>For E10 (standard unleaded), the IQR of <strong>{fmt_ppl(price_stats.loc['E10', 'iqr'])} ppl</strong> suggests moderate variation — consumers can save by shopping around, but most prices cluster within a reasonable band.</li>
        <li>For B7_STANDARD (standard diesel), the IQR of <strong>{fmt_ppl(price_stats.loc['B7_STANDARD', 'iqr'])} ppl</strong> indicates a similar pattern to E10.</li>
        <li>Premium fuel types show wider spreads, which may reflect inconsistent pricing strategies or less competitive pressure on premium grades.</li>
        <li>The presence of extreme outliers (visible in the box plots) suggests some forecourts may be charging prices well above the market norm — possibly due to remote locations, limited competition, or data reporting anomalies.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<!-- ── UPDATE FRESHNESS ────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section class="section" id="update-freshness">
    <h2>4. Retailer Reporting Freshness Analysis</h2>
    <p>
        The timeliness of price updates is a critical measure of data quality and retailer compliance with
        the government's open reporting mandate. The lag between <code>source_updated_at</code> (when the
        retailer actually changed/confirmed the price) and <code>recorded_at</code> (when the system captured
        it) reveals how fresh the data is that consumers and analysts rely upon.
    </p>

    <div class="kpi-grid">
        <div class="kpi-card">
            <div class="kpi-value">{overall_lag_median:.1f}h</div>
            <div class="kpi-label">Median Update Lag</div>
        </div>
        <div class="kpi-card">
            <div class="kpi-value">{overall_lag_mean:.1f}h</div>
            <div class="kpi-label">Mean Update Lag</div>
        </div>
        <div class="kpi-card">
            <div class="kpi-value">{fmt_pct(pct_over_24)}</div>
            <div class="kpi-label">Records > 24h Lag</div>
        </div>
        <div class="kpi-card">
            <div class="kpi-value">{fmt_pct(pct_over_72)}</div>
            <div class="kpi-label">Records > 72h Lag</div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Lag Statistics by Fuel Type</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Fuel Type</th>
                    <th>Count</th>
                    <th>Mean (h)</th>
                    <th>Median (h)</th>
                    <th>Std Dev</th>
                    <th>Q75 (h)</th>
                    <th>Max (h)</th>
                    <th>% > 24h</th>
                    <th>% > 72h</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>{lag_table_rows}</tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <div class="chart">
        <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart_lag_hist}" alt="Distribution of update lag">
        <div class="chart-caption">Figure 6: Distribution of update lag across all records. The median lag is marked, along with 24h and 72h thresholds.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="chart">
        <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart_lag_box}" alt="Update lag by fuel type">
        <div class="chart-caption">Figure 7: Box plot of update lag by fuel type (outliers hidden for clarity). Orange and red lines mark 24h and 72h thresholds.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout {'alert' if stale_pct > 10 else 'warning'}">
        <p><strong>Data Freshness Concern:</strong> {fmt_pct(stale_pct)} of all records have an update lag
        exceeding 72 hours (3 days), and <strong>{int(stale_count):,}</strong> records are flagged as stale.
        Additionally, {very_stale:,} stations have at least one price record with a lag exceeding 7 days.
        {'This level of stale data raises concerns about the reliability of the price information available to consumers.' if stale_pct > 5 else 'While the majority of data is reasonably fresh, these outliers warrant attention.'}</p>
    </div>

    {"<div class=\"callout\"><p><strong>Note:</strong> " + f"{negative_lag_count:,} records ({fmt_pct(pct_negative)}) show a negative lag (source_updated_at after recorded_at). This can occur when the source system timestamps are updated retroactively or due to clock synchronization differences between systems.</p></div>" if negative_lag_count > 0 else ""}

    <h3>Freshness Patterns</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>The median lag of <strong>{overall_lag_median:.1f} hours</strong> suggests that most price updates are captured within a reasonable timeframe.</li>
        <li>However, the mean lag of <strong>{overall_lag_mean:.1f} hours</strong> is significantly higher, pulled upward by a long tail of stale records — some with lags exceeding hundreds of hours.</li>
        <li>Stale data disproportionately affects certain stations, suggesting that a subset of retailers may not be updating their prices regularly as required by the reporting mandate.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<!-- ── STATION COVERAGE ────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section class="section" id="station-coverage">
    <h2>5. Station Coverage & Fuel Type Availability</h2>
    <p>
        Not every forecourt sells every fuel type. This section examines how many stations report each fuel
        type and the distribution of fuel offerings across stations — important context for understanding
        whether the data provides comprehensive market coverage or has significant gaps.
    </p>

    <h3>Fuel Type Coverage Across Stations</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Fuel Type</th>
                    <th>Stations Reporting</th>
                    <th>Total Records</th>
                    <th>% of All Stations</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>{coverage_table_rows}</tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <div class="chart">
        <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart_coverage}" alt="Station coverage by fuel type">
        <div class="chart-caption">Figure 8: Number of stations reporting each fuel type.</div>
    </div>

    <h3>Fuel Types per Station</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Fuel Types Reported</th>
                    <th>Number of Stations</th>
                    <th>% of Stations</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>{fuel_per_station_rows}</tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <div class="chart">
        <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart_fuel_per_station}" alt="Distribution of fuel types per station">
        <div class="chart-caption">Figure 9: How many fuel types each station reports. Most stations report 2–3 fuel types.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <p><strong>Coverage Insight:</strong> E10 and B7_STANDARD are the most widely reported fuel types,
        consistent with their status as the standard petrol and diesel grades mandated at UK forecourts.
        Premium and specialty fuels (E5, B7_PREMIUM, B10) have significantly lower station coverage,
        reflecting their niche market position. Stations reporting only a single fuel type may represent
        either limited product offerings or incomplete reporting.</p>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- ── KEY FINDINGS & POLICY IMPLICATIONS ──────────────────────────────── -->
<section class="section" id="key-findings">
    <h2>6. Key Findings & Policy Implications</h2>
    <p>
        The following findings synthesise the analysis above and highlight implications for fuel pricing
        policy, consumer protection, and market transparency.
    </p>

    <div class="finding">
        <span class="finding-num">1</span>
        <h4>Standard Fuel Prices Show Moderate but Meaningful Variation</h4>
        <p>
            For E10 (standard unleaded), prices range from <strong>{fmt_ppl(price_stats.loc['E10', 'min'])} to
            {fmt_ppl(price_stats.loc['E10', 'max'])} ppl</strong> with an IQR of
            <strong>{fmt_ppl(price_stats.loc['E10', 'iqr'])} ppl</strong>. B7_STANDARD (diesel) shows a similar
            pattern with an IQR of <strong>{fmt_ppl(price_stats.loc['B7_STANDARD', 'iqr'])} ppl</strong>.
            This means a consumer who shops around can realistically save
            {fmt_ppl(price_stats.loc['E10', 'iqr'])}+ pence per litre — a material difference for household budgets.
        </p>
        <div class="policy-note">Policy implication: Price comparison tools and transparency mandates should be maintained and promoted to help consumers benefit from available price differences.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="finding">
        <span class="finding-num">2</span>
        <h4>Premium Fuels Carry a Significant Price Premium with Wider Dispersion</h4>
        <p>
            {most_expensive_fuel} averages <strong>{fmt_ppl(most_exp_mean)} ppl</strong> — substantially above the
            standard grades. More importantly, premium fuel types exhibit wider IQRs, meaning consumers face
            less predictable pricing. The IQR for {largest_iqr_fuel} is
            <strong>{fmt_ppl(largest_iqr_val)} ppl</strong>, suggesting inconsistent pricing strategies or
            varying levels of competition in premium fuel markets.
        </p>
        <div class="policy-note">Policy implication: Regulators should examine whether premium fuel pricing is sufficiently transparent and whether consumers are adequately informed about the price differential versus standard grades.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="finding">
        <span class="finding-num">3</span>
        <h4>A Significant Minority of Price Records Are Stale</h4>
        <p>
            {fmt_pct(stale_pct)} of records have an update lag exceeding 72 hours, and
            {very_stale:,} stations have at least one record with a lag over 7 days. The median lag is
            {overall_lag_median:.1f}h, but the long right tail means some consumers may be relying on
            outdated price information.
        </p>
        <div class="policy-note">Policy implication: Enforcement of timely reporting should be strengthened. Stations consistently failing to update prices within 24–48 hours should be flagged, and consumers should be alerted when price data may be stale.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="finding">
        <span class="finding-num">4</span>
        <h4>Market Coverage Is Broad for Standard Fuels but Sparse for Premiums</h4>
        <p>
            E10 and B7_STANDARD are reported by the vast majority of stations, providing strong market
            visibility. However, premium fuels (E5, B7_PREMIUM, B10) have significantly lower station
            coverage, limiting the usefulness of price comparisons for these grades.
        </p>
        <div class="policy-note">Policy implication: Coverage requirements for the open reporting mandate should be reviewed to ensure all commonly available fuel types are consistently reported.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="finding">
        <span class="finding-num">5</span>
        <h4>Extreme Price Outliers May Indicate Market Failures or Reporting Errors</h4>
        <p>
            The max−min range for {largest_range_fuel} is <strong>{fmt_ppl(largest_range_val)} ppl</strong>.
            While some variation is expected, extreme outliers at the upper end could indicate stations in
            areas with no competition exploiting their market position, or alternatively, data entry errors.
        </p>
        <div class="policy-note">Policy implication: Automated anomaly detection should be implemented to flag prices that deviate significantly from the local or national distribution. Such outliers should be verified and, if accurate, investigated for potential anti-competitive behaviour.</div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- ── METHODOLOGY NOTE ────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section class="section">
    <h2>Methodology & Data Notes</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>All statistics are computed directly from the raw dataset (<code>data.csv</code>, {total_records:,} records). No imputation, interpolation, or fabrication has been applied.</li>
        <li>Prices are in pence per litre (ppl). The dataset includes both integer and decimal (0.9) price values.</li>
        <li>Update lag is calculated as <code>recorded_at − source_updated_at</code> in hours. Negative lags occur when the source timestamp is later than the recording timestamp, likely due to system synchronisation.</li>
        <li>Station identifiers are anonymised SHA-style hashes. No geographic, brand, or regional analysis is possible from this dataset alone.</li>
        <li>Outliers are retained in all calculations but are excluded from box plot visuals for readability.</li>
        <li>Only fuel types with more than 100 observations are included in the violin plot; all types are included in tabular and box plot analyses.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

</div>

